    "case_summary": "A teenage girl from Saint-Martin presented with bilateral blurred vision and ocular irritation. One month earlier, she experienced fever and severe arthralgia. She also presented with neurological complications and recently acquired periorbital hyperpigmentation. Her ocular history was negative for trauma and previous inflammation. Primary HIV infection, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, and syphilis had been ruled out.\n\nBest-corrected visual acuity was 20/20 OU. Slitlamp biomicroscopy revealed:- Bilateral punctate superficial keratitis- Stromal immune ring formation with underlying keratic precipitates - Mild anterior chamber reaction- Normal intraocular pressure- Normal fundus examination\n\n- Anterior segment optical coherence tomography: Confirmed localized anterior stromal infiltrate with underlying keratic precipitates and normal corneal thickness- Laboratory tests:  - Aqueous tap PCR results:    - Negative for human herpesviruses 1 and 2, varicella-zoster virus, cytomegalovirus, adenovirus, and rubella virus    - Positive for Chikungunya virus with weak viral load (detected by real-time PCR)  - Aqueous humor: Negative for CHIKV-specific immunoglobulins  - Positive IgM CHIKV serologic test (from initial diagnosis)- Images:  Figure 1A Title: Fluorescein Staining of the Right Eye  Description: Showing inferior superficial punctate keratitis  Figure 1B Title: Slitlamp Photograph of the Right Eye  Description: Showing stromal immune ring formation  Figure 2A Title: Slitlamp Photograph of the Right Eye  Description: Showing keratic precipitates  Figure 2B&C Title: Infrared Photograph and Anterior Segment OCT of the Right Eye  Description: Showing localized anterior stromal infiltrate with normal corneal thickness",
